  down in the notifications spec.

  [1mBut there's a harder constraint I want to surface before we go further, because it may change the answer you just [0m
  [1mgave.[0m

  A single-user app with no server fundamentally cannot reach you when you're not in it. That's the central tension in
  notifying a local-first app: the reminder you most want ("this is due tomorrow") is exactly the one that requires
  something to be running when you aren't looking.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a badge and an inbox you see when the page is open. Free, works today with local storage. But it only
    tells you things when you're already looking at your tasks, which is when you'd have noticed anyway. Rules out
    nothing later.
  - [1mService worker + browser notifications[0m — real OS-level popups that can fire with the tab closed, still no server,
    data stays on the device. Costs a service worker and a permission prompt. Limits: only while the browser is running,
    and only on that one device. Cheap to add later, but it shapes how reminders get scheduled.
  - [1mServer-backed push or email[0m — reaches you on any device, anytime. Genuinely solves the problem. But it pulls the
    backend forward into sub-project 1, which is the thing you just deferred.

  My recommendation is the [1mservice worker[0m path. It's the only option that delivers actual value without a backend, and
  the in-app inbox gets built underneath it either way — so it's additive, not a fork you'd have to undo. I'd only go
  server-backed now if reaching you on your phone is the actual point of the feature.
